utton><div class="banner-options u-padding-xs-bottom"><button class="button-link AddToMendeley button-link-secondary u-margin-s-right u-display-inline-flex-from-md button-link-icon-left button-link-has-colored-icon" type="button"><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 86 128" height="20" class="icon icon-plus"><path d="M48 58V20H38v38H0v10h38v38h10V68h38V58z"></path></svg><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Add to Mendeley</span></span></button><div class="Social u-display-inline-block" id="social"><div class="popover social-popover" id="social-popover"><div id="popover-trigger-social-popover"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary u-margin-s-right button-link-icon-left button-link-has-colored-icon" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-haspopup="true"><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 114 128" height="20" class="icon icon-share"><path d="M90 112c-6.62 0-12-5.38-12-12s5.38-12 12-12 12 5.38 12 12-5.38 12-12 12zM24 76c-6.62 0-12-5.38-12-12s5.38-12 12-12 12 5.38 12 12-5.38 12-12 12zm66-60c6.62 0 12 5.38 12 12s-5.38 12-12 12-12-5.38-12-12 5.38-12 12-12zm0 62c-6.56 0-12.44 2.9-16.48 7.48L45.1 70.2c.58-1.98.9-4.04.9-6.2s-.32-4.22-.9-6.2l28.42-15.28C77.56 47.1 83.44 50 90 50c12.14 0 22-9.86 22-22S102.14 6 90 6s-22 9.86-22 22c0 1.98.28 3.9.78 5.72L40.14 49.1C36.12 44.76 30.38 42 24 42 11.86 42 2 51.86 2 64s9.86 22 22 22c6.38 0 12.12-2.76 16.14-7.12l28.64 15.38c-.5 1.84-.78 3.76-.78 5.74 0 12.14 9.86 22 22 22s22-9.86 22-22-9.86-22-22-22z"></path></svg><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Share</span></span></button></div></div></div><div class="ExportCitation u-display-inline-block" id="export-citation"><div class="popover export-citation-popover" id="export-citation-popover"><div id="popover-trigger-export-citation-popover"><button class="button-link button-link-secondary button-link-icon-left button-link-has-colored-icon" type="button" aria-expanded="false" aria-haspopup="true"><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 104 128" height="20" class="icon icon-cited-by-66"><path d="M2 58.78V106h44V64H12v-5.22C12 40.28 29.08 32 46 32V22C20.1 22 2 37.12 2 58.78zM102 32V22c-25.9 0-44 15.12-44 36.78V106h44V64H68v-5.22C68 40.28 85.08 32 102 32z"></path></svg><span class="button-link-text-container"><span class="button-link-text">Cite</span></span></button></div></div></div></div></div><div class="ArticleIdentifierLinks u-margin-xs-bottom text-xs" id="article-identifier-links"><a class="anchor doi anchor-primary" href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asoc.2021.107318" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener" aria-label="Persistent link using digital object identifier" title="Persistent link using digital object identifier"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">https://doi.org/10.1016/j.asoc.2021.107318</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a><a class="anchor rights-and-content anchor-primary" href="https://s100.copyright.com/AppDispatchServlet?publisherName=ELS&amp;contentID=S1568494621002416&amp;orderBeanReset=true" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer noopener"><span class="anchor-text-container"><span class="anchor-text">Get rights and content</span><svg focusable="false" viewBox="0 0 8 8" height="20" aria-label="Opens in new window" class="icon icon-arrow-up-right-tiny arrow-external-link"><path d="M1.12949 2.1072V1H7V6.85795H5.89111V2.90281L0.784057 8L0 7.21635L5.11902 2.1072H1.12949Z"></path></svg></span></a></div><section class="ReferencedArticles"></section><section class="ReferencedArticles"></section><div id="preview-section-abstract"><div class="PageDivider"></div><div class="Abstracts u-font-serif" id="abstracts"><div class="abstract author-highlights" id="d1e2013"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Highlights</h2><div id="d1e2016"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2017"><ul class="list"><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">‚Ä¢</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2023">An Optimize GA for Cluster Head Selection protocol is proposed for IoT enabled WSN.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">‚Ä¢</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2028">To alleviate hot-spot problem, multiple movable sinks in network are incorporated.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">‚Ä¢</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2033">A new deployment strategy is considered for optimization of network energy.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">‚Ä¢</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2038">Performance evaluation of OptiGACHS variants is done against the GAOC and MS-GAOC.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">‚Ä¢</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2043">OptiGACHS variants are developed for unattended hostile applications.</div></span></li></ul></div></div></div><div class="abstract author" id="d1e2005"><h2 class="section-title u-h4 u-margin-l-top u-margin-xs-bottom">Abstract</h2><div id="d1e2008"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2009"><span>The most crucial design constraint on the <a href="/topics/engineering/internet-of-things" title="Learn more about Internet of Things from ScienceDirect's AI-generated Topic Pages" class="topic-link">Internet of Things</a> (IoT) enabled </span><a href="/topics/engineering/wireless-sensor-network" title="Learn more about wireless sensor networks from ScienceDirect's AI-generated Topic Pages" class="topic-link">wireless sensor networks</a><span><span> (WSNs) is <a href="/topics/engineering/energy-dissipation" title="Learn more about energy dissipation from ScienceDirect's AI-generated Topic Pages" class="topic-link">energy dissipation</a>. The inefficient data collection by the resource constraint sensors becomes a major roadblock in energy preservation to achieve network longevity. The energy of nodes has to be utilized in an efficient manner which helps in increasing the longevity of WSNs. Clustering is a technique that can utilize the energy of the sensors efficiently by maintaining load balancing among the sensors for increasing the lifetime and scalability of the networks. In this paper, the energy consumption of the network is improved by considering the </span><a href="/topics/engineering/genetic-algorithm" title="Learn more about Genetic Algorithm from ScienceDirect's AI-generated Topic Pages" class="topic-link">Genetic Algorithm</a><span> evolutionary computing technique. The proposed OptiGACHS protocol describes the improved cluster head (CH) <a href="/topics/engineering/selection-procedure" title="Learn more about selection procedure from ScienceDirect's AI-generated Topic Pages" class="topic-link">selection procedure</a> by incorporating criteria of distance, density, energy, and heterogeneous node‚Äôs capability for developing fitness function. The OptiGACHS protocol operates with single, multiple static, and multiple movable sinks to have an impartial comparative examination. Multiple movable sinks are proposed to shorten transmission distance between the sink and CH and also pact with the hot-spot problem. A deployment strategy for nodes is also discussed for energy and distance optimization during network operation. It is observed from simulations that the proposed OptiGACHS protocol outperforms existing protocols.</span></span></div></div></div></div></div><div id="preview-section-introduction"><div class="PageDivider"></div><div class="Introduction u-font-serif u-margin-l-ver"><h2 class="u-h4 u-margin-s-bottom">Introduction</h2><section id="sec1"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2070">Nowadays, worldwide researchers are considering variouspromising technologies such as the Internet of Things (IoTs), Fog &amp; Cloud Computing and Big Data for solving computationally expensive real-life problems. These emerging as well as promising technologies are being applied in various fields such as smart surveillance, smart city, infrastructure, water distribution, automated transportation, urban security and environmental monitoring, healthcare, smarter energy management systems and other industrial necessities for providing efficient solutions. But among all technologies, IoTs is one of the keystone achievements which makes a huge impact on modern life by connecting things with the Internet. The increment in the IoT infrastructure requires a dense structure of IoT enabled wireless sensor networks (WSN)&nbsp;[1]. The IoTs enabled WSNs are a group of spatially distributed battery-operated sensors that are dedicated to performing a pre-defined task. These sensor nodes are capable of monitoring various environmental conditions such as moisture, temperature, vibration, etc. which are recorded, processed and communicated wirelessly to a central location called a sink. The IoT enabled WSNs can be divided into different categories depending on the application domain for which they are designed, such as event-driven WSNs (sensor nodes get activated only when some event occurs), query-driven WSNs (sensor nodes get activated only to respond to a user-generated query), the periodic update is driven WSNs (sensor nodes send the collected data at periodic intervals to the user), etc. The IoT enabled WSNs are exploited in numerous domains such as military applications, agricultural monitoring, home automation, industrial control, etc. These networks offer the ability of self-configuration allowing them to work in unattended, remote, and hostile environments&nbsp;[2], [3].</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2082">Although the IoT enabled WSNs have brought unprecedented opportunities in different application domains, they are vulnerable to some of the resource constraints. The most important resource in a given IoT enabled WSN is the energy of nodes which is required in many tasks of IoT enabled WSN i.e.&nbsp;monitoring, recording, processing, and communicating. As the sensor nodes are equipped with a limited battery, they suffer from energy drainage which can further lead to a whole network shutdown. Since in remote or hostile environments, it is not possible to replace the batteries; much of the research work in the field of IoT enabled WSNs is focused on increasing the lifetime of the network. It has been observed that the longevity of the IoT enabled WSN is determined by the energy depletion rate of the sensor nodes. The faster the energy depletion rate, the smaller would be the lifetime of the network&nbsp;[3], [4], [5].</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2088">To address the issue of energy depletion, the concept of clustering was introduced. The idea of clustering is to group the sensors and elect a cluster head (CH) as the representative of each cluster. It is designed with the sole aim of energy preservation where the task of data communication from sensor nodes to the sink, is delegated to the CHs. The earliest work to a clustering algorithm is Low Energy Adaptive Clustering Hierarchy (LEACH) protocol which was specifically designed for homogeneous networks&nbsp;[6]. In homogeneous networks, all nodes are assumed to have the same capability in terms of energy and processing power. But, the existence of homogeneity in nodes is not always possible. Many improvements of the LEACH protocol have been proposed which can significantly increase the network lifetime. However, the application of clustering protocols in heterogeneous networks, particularly the heterogeneity in terms of energy, needs to be considered. In the clustering-based protocols, the election of CH is itself a big concern. In a typical clustering algorithm, the selection of CH is based on some parameters, and every sensor in a cluster passes its data to the corresponding CH. The CH is responsible for aggregating the collected data to remove redundancy and communicate the data to the sink. It is to be noted that one of the major energy-depleting elements is the wireless radio. This implies that the CH is vulnerable to more energy drainage issues as compared to other sensor nodes in the cluster. If a CH goes out of energy, the functioning of the whole cluster would be disturbed which can impact the lifetime of the network&nbsp;[6], [7], [8], [9].</div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2098">To handle the above-mentioned issues, an optimized approach for the selection of CH is required. Many researchers have put forward their proposed strategies using metaheuristic approaches for optimizing the CH selection. Their research aims to develop an optimized selection of CH that minimizes energy consumption. However, the problem of the selection of optimal CH towards energy-efficient routing is a non-deterministic polynomial-time hard (NP-Hard) problem. Nevertheless, the selection of CH can be optimized by employing metaheuristics incorporating some key factors of CH selection in the processing of building the fitness function. Some of the metaheuristic techniques such as genetic algorithm (GA), artificial bee colony (ABC), particle swarm optimization (PSO), etc. have been utilized for CH selection that focuses on energy saving of nodes. Another factor that is responsible for the energy consumption of the nodes in the communication through a wireless medium&nbsp;[10]. Generally, the transmission power of a node decays in proportionality to the distance between the communicating nodes. Applications with large regions of interest can cause energy exhaustion at a faster rate if single-hop communication is used. Therefore, using multi-hop communication seems like a rescue for such energy depletion. But, multi-hop transmission causes energy depletion of relaying nodes that are exhausted of their energy resources gradually over time. This leads to a ‚Äòno connection zone‚Äô called ‚Äòhotspot‚Äô that is a region in which the sensor nodes are completely exhausted. To alleviate and diminish the hotspot problem, the proposed system is incorporated with movable sinks inside and outside the region to ameliorate the distance factor for communication.</div><section id="sec1.1"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2109">Genetic algorithm-based methods are suitable for large IoT enabled WSN areas for data communication and formulate energy-efficient clusters for data aggregation. Initially, the protocol randomly selects cluster head (CH) from homogeneous and heterogeneous sensor nodes and then gradually shift to efficient solutions. Generally, most of the Genetic algorithm-based protocols do not algorithmically take advantage of network heterogeneity. Since the algorithm is of heuristic nature where any combination of chromosomes can result in best fitness value, a normal node can be selected as CH despite the presence of an intermediate or advanced node in the cluster. If such a situation occurs in setup phase, it can lead to instability; as a normal energy node will not be able to hold the cluster for a long time. To overcome this, the proposed protocol not only considers energy but also considers comparative hardware capabilities as a parameter (bias) for evaluation and efficient selection of CH. This way, most powerful nodes in the network have a higher chance of being selected as CH. The residual energy, node density and distance optimization are also taken care of for the network‚Äôs longevity. The CH to sink communication distance is optimized by introducing movable sinks. The sink movement benefits the clusters to choose closest of the available sinks and transmit data to it, which also promotes distribution and balancing the load onto sinks.</div></section><section id="sec1.2"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2132"><ul class="list"><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">(1)</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2138"><em>Proposes a new clustering approach using GA</em>: This work proposes an Optimized Genetic Algorithm for Cluster Head Selection (OptiGACHS) protocol for CH selection by incorporating criterion of distance, density, energy, and heterogeneous node‚Äôs capability for its developed fitness function. Since GA is a heuristic technique, it eases the cognitive load of CH decision making by formulating its fitness function based on carefully chosen parameters. Thus, this metaheuristic approach brings unprecedented network structures for unintended hostile environments.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">(2)</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2145"><em>Introduces a deployment strategy for energy optimization:</em> In this work, a deployment strategy is also introduced for positioning heterogeneous sensors in a well-organized way. The sensors are deployed in the region of interest according to a coherent logical structure. During network operation, the optimally placed sensor node with respect to sinks minimizes energy consumption. The strategy also supervises the unnecessary closely bound deployment of high energy nodes.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">(3)</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2152"><em>Incorporates multiple movable sinks inside and outside network region to alleviate the hot-spot problem:</em> Both static and movable sinks are employed in different scenarios of OptiGACHS protocol outside the network area to diminish the hot-spot problem. A single sink scenario is also introduced following the same parameters for CH selection as defined for multiple sinks. For ameliorating distance factor, sinks move in predefined trajectory outside the network area and CH chooses the closest sink available for communication.</div></span></li><li class="react-xocs-list-item"><span class="list-label">(4)</span><span class="list-content"><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2159"><em>Performance evaluation of proposed approach with state of the art protocols:</em> Performance of OptiGACHS protocol is assessed against the state of the art protocols on some metrics viz. network lifetime, stability period, remaining energy, and throughput. Moreover, to contemplate the essence of movable data sinks outside the network area, performance evaluation of multiple static sinks is done against multiple movable sinks.</div></span></li></ul></div><div class="u-margin-s-bottom" id="d1e2163">The rest of the paper is organized as follows: Section&nbsp;2 discusses the comprehensive literature review of the GA based clustering techniques. Section&nbsp;3 explains the system model which includes the network model and radio energy dissipation model. The OptiGACHS proposed protocol is discussed in Section&nbsp;4. The simulation results and discussions are presented in Section&nbsp;5 and the paper is concluded in Section&nbsp;6 with its future works.</div></section></section></div></div><div class="hor-line-top u-padding-m-bottom u-padding-m-top u-text-center u-position-relative inserted-access-block">
